# Accepted infrastructure findings, each with the reason it is accepted.
# Everything not listed here is a real finding: fix it rather than adding a line.

# The Kubernetes API server endpoint is publicly reachable.
# Reason: the CI runners that deploy this project have no fixed egress address,
# so an allowlist would break every deploy. Access is authenticated with IAM
# and every call is written to the control-plane audit log (see eks.tf).
# Revisit when moving to self-hosted runners with a static NAT address.
AVD-AWS-0040
AVD-AWS-0041

# Security group rules allow unrestricted egress (0.0.0.0/0).
# Reason: worker nodes must reach the EKS control plane, ECR, and the AWS APIs
# they call, and there is no NAT gateway or VPC endpoint set to narrow that to.
# Egress-any on a node security group is the default posture of every managed
# Kubernetes setup; INGRESS is where this blueprint is strict, and the database
# security group has no egress rules at all. Replace with VPC endpoints plus a
# scoped egress list when outbound filtering becomes a requirement, and delete
# this line at the same time.
AVD-AWS-0104

# ECR repository tags are mutable.
# Reason: a retried deploy pushes the same commit-sha tag, and an immutable
# repository rejects it — turning every retry into a failed build. Provenance
# still holds: the tag IS the commit sha, and the image is scanned on push.
# Revisit if you move to digest-pinned deploys, where immutability is free.
AVD-AWS-0031

# Worker nodes run in public subnets with public IPs.
# Reason: nodes reach ECR and the control plane over their own interface, which
# removes a NAT gateway from the bill and the failure path. Inbound traffic is
# restricted to the cluster security group; the load balancer is the only
# intentional entry point. Revisit when egress filtering becomes a requirement.
AVD-AWS-0164
